    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's a helpful breakdown. I'm still a little confused about the \"blocking\" part though. Can you explain it in a way that makes the enzyme-substrate interaction easier to visualize? Like, how does the inhibitor actually physically prevent the enzyme from working on Angiotensin I?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Imagine ACE as a lock where Angiotensin I is the key. When Angiotensin I enters the lock (enzyme active site), the enzyme undergoes a conformational change, facilitating the cleavage of the substrate and producing Angiotensin II. An ACE inhibitor essentially fits into the lock but doesn't resemble the shape of the Angiotensin I key—it would look like it is a decoy key designed not to turn in the lock. This decoy prevents the actual key (Angiotensin I) from fitting properly, and therefore, the lock (ACE enzyme) doesn't undergo the conformational change to alter the key (Angiotensin I) into Angiotensin II.\n\n\nIn the context of an ACE inhibitor, instead of angiotensin I getting converted into angiotensin II, it's simply left as is—or more accurately, as \"Angiotensin I 1 (inactive),\" and the RAAS pathway is interrupted before it can exert all of its effects on blood pressure regulation. This mechanism is what ultimately leads to the blood pressure-lowering effects associated with ACE inhibitors like Lisinopril. So, the inhibitor \"blocks\" the enzyme's function by competing for the active site and preventing the normal conversion of Angiotensin I to Angiot",
      "source": "target",
